    \u0430\u043b\u0433\u0435\u0431\u0440\u044b. \u0422\u0435\u043e\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0430 \u0425\u0430\u043d\u0430-\u0411\u0430\u043d\u0430\u0445\u0430
    \u043e \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0434\u043e\u043b\u0436\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0438 \u043b\u0438\u043d\u0435\u0439\u043d\u044b\u0445
    \u0444\u0443\u043d\u043a\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043e\u0432. \u0422\u0435\u043e\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0430
    \u0411\u0430\u043d\u0430\u0445\u0430-\u0428\u0442\u0435\u0439\u043d\u0433\u0430\u0443\u0437\u0430
    (\u043f\u0440\u0438\u043d\u0446\u0438\u043f \u0440\u0430\u0432\u043d\u043e\u043c\u0435\u0440\u043d\u043e\u0439
    \u043e\u0433\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0447\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438). \u0422\u0435\u043e\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0430
    \u0411\u0430\u043d\u0430\u0445\u0430 \u043e \u043d\u0435\u043f\u043e\u0434\u0432\u0438\u0436\u043d\u043e\u0439
    \u0442\u043e\u0447\u043a\u0435. \u041f\u0430\u0440\u0430\u0434\u043e\u043a\u0441 \u0411\u0430\u043d\u0430\u0445\u0430-\u0422\u0430\u0440\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0433\u043e
    (\u0442\u0435\u043e\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0430 \u043e \u0440\u0430\u0437\u0431\u0438\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0438
    \u0448\u0430\u0440\u0430). \u0415\u0433\u043e \u0440\u0430\u0431\u043e\u0442\u044b \u043e\u043f\u0440\u0435\u0434\u0435\u043b\u0438\u043b\u0438
    \u043b\u0438\u0446\u043e \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0433\u043e
    \u0430\u043d\u0430\u043b\u0438\u0437\u0430 \u043d\u0430 \u0434\u0435\u0441\u044f\u0442\u0438\u043b\u0435\u0442\u0438\u044f
    \u0432\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0434. \u0428\u0442\u0435\u0439\u043d\u0433\u0430\u0443\u0437
    \u043d\u0430\u0437\u044b\u0432\u0430\u043b \u0411\u0430\u043d\u0430\u0445\u0430 \u0441\u0432\u043e\u0438\u043c
    \u00ab\u0432\u0435\u043b\u0438\u0447\u0430\u0439\u0448\u0438\u043c \u043d\u0430\u0443\u0447\u043d\u044b\u043c
    \u043e\u0442\u043a\u0440\u044b\u0442\u0438\u0435\u043c\u00bb."
  shortBioEn: "Stefan Banah (1892–1945) is one of the most influential mathematicians in the 20th century, the founder of modern functional analysis and the key figure of the famous Lion Mathematical School, and his biography is an amazing story of a self-scientific who revolutionized mathematics and survived the horrors of World War II.\n\nEarly years and the way to the science of Origin: Stefan Banah was born on 30 March 1892 in Krakow (then Austro-Hungary). He was an extramarital son of Katažina Banah and a soldier of Stefan Grecek, and his father was unable to marry his mother, and a young Stephen was placed in the foster home of Francis Plova. He never knew his biological mother, and his father, although maintaining a bond, kept her identity secret for a long time.Education: At the school of Banah, he showed outstanding math skills, often with his friend, Vitold Wilkosh, a future mathematician, who went to Lviv Polytechnic School in 1910 to study engineering because he sincerely believed that there was nothing new in mathematics.Fateful meeting: During World War I, in 1916, a meeting took place in Krakow Park that changed Banah's life, and he and his friend Otto Nicodem talked about mathematics, and they were heard by a famous professor, Hugo Steingaus, who was struck by the depths of Banah's self-scientific mind, and Steingaus became his mentor and friend.Within a week, Banah had accomplished a difficult task that the professor had failed to accomplish and they published a joint work.\n\n♪ The Lviv period and the flourishing of the career is a professor and a school creator: With Steingauz's support, Banah was promoted to the position of assistant at Lviv Polytechnics in 1920 and later, in 1922, he defended his Ph.D. and became a professor, and since 1924 he has been a professor at the Lviv University of Jan Kazimir.The birth of functional analysis: Banah's main contribution to science was the creation and systematic development of the theory of banana spaces (full normal linear spaces), his 1920 thesis and the book \"Theory of Liner Operations\" (1932) laid the foundation for a new division of mathematics, functional analysis. \"Scotland Café\": Banah was the soul of a lion's mathematical school.He and his colleagues (Mazur, Ulam, Steingaus, etc.) gathered at the Scottish Café, where they discussed and solved the most difficult problems, and there was a famous Scottish Book, a thick notebook for recording tasks, and Banah preferred to work at the coffee shop table, often in a noisy environment, which did not prevent its incredible concentration.\n\nWar, Survival and Recent Years of the Soviet Period: In 1939, after the accession of Western Ukraine to the USSR, Banah became the Dean of the Faculty of Physics and Mathematics of the University of Lviv and a member of the United Nations System of Soviets (this status was later questioned) and maintained good relations with Soviet mathematicians.Nazi occupation: With the arrival of the Nazis in 1941, the university was closed down and the lives of Polish intellectuals were threatened. Banah, like many of his colleagues, saved the work of Professor Rudolph Waigle's rash feeder at the Institute for rash typhoids, the task was to feed the blood of the lice that was used to produce the vaccine.Death: After Lviv's release in 1944, Banah returned to teaching and rebuilding the university and planned to move to Krakow University, Yagellon, but these plans were not expected to come true; in January 1945, he was diagnosed with lung cancer, which he died on 31 August 1945, and was buried in the Lviv Cemetery of Ljachak.\n\nThe name Banah has dozens of fundamental concepts and theorems in mathematics, including: the Banahov Spaces and the Banahov Algebra; the Hana-Banach Theorem on the continuation of linear functions; the Banah-Steingauza Theorem (the principle of equal limitation); the Banah Theorem on the fixed point; the Banaha-Tarsky Paradox (theorem of breaking the ball); his work has been identified by a person of mathematical analysis for decades to come.Steingaus called Banah his \"greatest scientific discovery.\""
  interestingFactsRu: "\u0411\u0438\u043e\u0433\u0440\u0430\u0444\u0438\u044f \u0421\u0442\u0435\u0444\u0430\u043d\u0430
